---
import Main from "@/layouts/Main.astro";
import { siteSettings } from "@/config/siteSettings";

const siteName = siteSettings.branding.siteName;
---

<Main title={`About | ${siteName}`}>
  <section data-bible-autolink>
    <h1>About {siteName}</h1>
    <p>
      {siteName} is a web edition of <em
        >Catechism of Suffering: The God Who Comforts and Conforms</em
      >, by Matthew Statler. It presents the catechism as a searchable, browsable
      question-and-answer reference so the material is easier to read, teach, review, and revisit.
    </p>

    <h2>From the author&apos;s introduction</h2>

    <p>
      &ldquo;Thank you for choosing this booklet. Christians throughout the centuries have used
      catechisms as a tool to strengthen their faith and grow in their knowledge of God. The
      practice of question-and-answer helps anchor the heart in the truths that sustain us through
      every season of life.
    </p>

    <p>
      I have designed this catechism primarily for myself. As a pastor and a fellow sufferer, I need
      to often rehearse these truths to myself. They remind me that the path of suffering is not
      wasted, random, or meaningless. Suffering is governed by a wise and loving Father who uses
      affliction to conform His children to the image of His Son.&rdquo;
    </p>

    <h2>How to use this catechism</h2>

    <p>
      &ldquo;Suffering has a way of disorienting the soul. Pain clouds what once was clear when it
      was calm. The truths we confessed in strength can feel distant when sorrow comes. This
      catechism is written to steady your heart by retraining your mind, to lead you back again and
      again to the unshakable foundation of God&apos;s Word. In a sense, it is a training manual for
      your heart.
    </p>

    <p>
      The form is simple: question and answer. It is meant to help you think biblically about
      affliction. To speak truth to your soul and to find enduring comfort in the character of God.
      These brief statements are not a substitute for Scripture; they are a doorway back into
      it.&rdquo;
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Read one or two questions at a time. Maybe a section a week.</li>
      <li>Look up the Scripture proofs and linger over them.</li>
      <li>Pray the truths back to God in your own words.</li>
      <li>Return to them often, especially in times of suffering.</li>
      <li>Use the added space for your own notes and reminders.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      &ldquo;You may use this catechism privately, as a family, or in the context of counseling.
      However it is used, let the questions serve as gentle companions in your suffering, guiding
      you toward the God who draws near to the brokenhearted and who sanctifies His people through
      every trial. If you find this tool helpful, please pass it on to another sufferer who might
      benefit from these reminders in their hour of need.
    </p>

    <p>
      May this small work teach you to say with quiet confidence, even in the darkest hour:&rdquo;
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        &ldquo;I know, O Lord, that your rules are righteous, and that in faithfulness you have
        afflicted me.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p><cite>&mdash; Psalm 119:75</cite></p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      <em>Soli Deo Gloria,</em><br />
      <strong>Matthew Statler</strong>
    </p>

    <h2>About this edition</h2>

    <p>
      This site presents all sixty questions in the author&apos;s six sections, with the Scripture
      proofs kept under every answer.
    </p>

    <p>
      Credit is due to <strong>Matthew Statler</strong> for writing the
      <em>Catechism of Suffering</em>, and to <strong>Michael Coughlin</strong> for providing the website
      this edition is built on.
    </p>

    <p>
      The complete booklet is free to read online or download as a PDF from
      <a
        href="https://books.freely.giving/books/matthew-statler/catechism-of-suffering/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">books.freely.giving</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</Main>
